The XORcyst is a set of tools for 6502 software development: the `xasm`
assembler and `xlnk` linker. This release is version 1.9.10.
Run `xasm --version` or `xlnk --version` to identify an installed build.

Build and test
--------------

Normal builds use the checked-in parser, scanner, man pages, and configure
script. A C compiler, make, and a POSIX shell are required; Bison, Flex, and
help2man are not required.
On macOS, the command-line developer tools provide the CoreFoundation framework
used for filesystem path comparison. The test suite also requires Python 3.

    ./configure --disable-maintainer-mode
    make -j2
    make check

Use `make install` to install the tools. See INSTALL for prefix and compiler
options. `make installcheck` exercises the installed tools. CI runs
`make distcheck` on Linux and macOS: it builds an unpacked source archive, runs
the complete regression suite, and checks installation and uninstallation.
Symbol-table, AST/JSON, malformed-input, and output-failure tests also run under
ASan/UBSan on Linux. Tests use generated
fixtures and do not require game ROMs or a NESrev checkout.

See xorcyst.texinfo and the doc/ directory for the assembly language and linker
script reference. `xasm --help` lists all current command-line options, as do
the installed `man xasm` / `man xlnk` pages.

Assembly and verification
-------------------------

    xasm game.asm -o game.o
    xasm --pure-binary game.asm -o game.prg

Object output is intended for `xlnk`; pure-binary output requires all addresses
to be resolved by the assembler. To check a raw binary against a reference:

    xasm --pure-binary game.asm -o game.prg --compare=reference.prg

For a reference containing a header or other regions, use `--compare-offset`
and `--compare-length`. `--compare-format=json` produces a structured report;
`--compare-max-mismatches` limits the displayed mismatches.
`--compare-cpu-base=ADDR` supplies a fallback CPU base for mismatches without
an address from the assembler's source map.

Listings and cross-references
-----------------------------

`--listing=FILE` supports `--listing-format=text|json|ndjson` (default text).
Structured listings default to version 1. Use `--listing-version=2` with
`--pure-binary` and JSON or NDJSON for explicit segment and emission metadata;
see the listing contract below. Version 1 and text output retain their existing
format, including version 1's numeric offsets on non-emitting rows and its
omission of numeric-only `.char` data from bytes and address accounting.
Version 2 corrects both limitations.
`--xref=FILE` supports `--xref-format=json|text|csv` (default JSON). CSV expands
the supplied prefix into `.symbols.csv` and `.refs.csv` destinations.

A complete JSON xref, including local/anonymous symbols, data references,
lexical owners, and structured instruction records:

    xasm --pure-binary game.asm -o game.prg \
      --xref=game.xref.json --xref-include-locals=true \
      --xref-include-anon=true --xref-data=true \
      --xref-include-owner=true --xref-instructions=true \
      --listing=game.listing.json --listing-format=json

The optional instruction stream includes every emitted instruction, independent
of xref symbol filters. It preserves source spans, pre-fold expression trees,
and final instruction bytes, and gives each instruction's memory access and the
signed terms its operand is built from. Lexical owners do not prove control
flow.

To write instruction records separately:

    xasm --pure-binary game.asm -o game.prg \
      --instruction-records-output=game.instructions.json \
      --dependency-manifest=game.dependencies.json

The standalone instruction output requires a dependency manifest.
A manifest records consumed-input hashes, lookup misses, invocation arguments,
and producer identity. It is published last, after input revalidation. Consumers
must still validate their own configuration and output identity when reusing
artifacts. See XASM_INSTRUCTION_RECORDS_V3_SPEC.md,
XASM_INSTRUCTION_RECORDS_V2_SPEC.md, XASM_INSTRUCTION_RECORDS_SPEC.md,
XASM_DATA_DIRECTIVE_REFERENCES_SPEC.md, and XASM_DEPENDENCY_MANIFEST_SPEC.md.

Upgrading from 1.7.0: instruction records now use schema version 3. Consumers
must resolve each span's integer `file` through the document's `files` table
and handle the memory-access and additive-term facts introduced in version 2.
There is no switch to emit an older records schema. Access classifications in
xrefs, summaries, index patterns, and data consumers also change; see the
version 2 specification for corrected reads, writes, and immediate references.

FCEUX debugger names
-------------------

    xasm --pure-binary game.asm -o game.prg \
      --fceux-nl-rom-prefix=game.nes. \
      --fceux-nl-ram-output=game.nes.ram.nl

Use the reference ROM's pathname followed by a dot as the ROM prefix. Output
files are named `game.nes.0.nl`, `game.nes.1.nl`, and so on, using uppercase
hexadecimal physical 16 KiB PRG page numbers. Put these files beside that ROM.
The assembled binary must contain the complete raw PRG payload, without an iNES
header or CHR data. Unlabeled pages still receive empty files.

NL export includes local labels independently of xref flags. RAM names come
from dataseg labels and named direct memory operands below $8000; immediate-only
constants and anonymous operands are excluded. For a 16 KiB NROM program, add
`--fceux-nl-mirror-16k` to name both CPU windows. This option requires exactly
16384 emitted bytes. Higher-numbered files from earlier builds are not deleted;
the build process should manage obsolete sidecars. See XASM_FCEUX_NL_EXPORT_SPEC.md.

Analysis and diagnostics
------------------------

Optional analyses include `--xref-summary`, `--analyze-index-patterns`,
`--data-consumers`, and `--analyze-data-coverage`. Each supports text, JSON, and
NDJSON output. The corresponding destination flags are `--xref-summary-output`,
`--index-patterns-output`, `--data-consumers-output`, and `--data-coverage-output`.
Without a destination, these analyses write to stdout. When combining analyses,
give each one an explicit output path. See XASM_INDEX_BOUND_ANALYSIS_SPEC.md for
the evidence and limits of index-bound inference.

Unused EQU diagnostics are controlled by `--warn-unused-equ`,
`--Werror=unused-equ`, and `--Wno-unused-equ`. The raw-address audit uses
`--audit-raw-addresses`, `--audit-level=warn|error`,
`--audit-rom-range=LO-HI`, and `--audit-output-format=text|json`.

See XASM_ANALYSIS_FEATURES_GUIDE.md for a worked walkthrough of the analysis
features above against one example program, with real command output and an
explanation of what each feature is for.

Output completion
-----------------

A failed invocation can leave some outputs updated. Check the exit status
before using generated artifacts; an output file's existence does not mean the
build succeeded. Diagnostic listings can still be produced for failed assemblies.

Exit statuses: 0 success; 1 assembly or binary-generation error; 2 CLI misuse;
3 destination validation or listing/xref/instruction-record/NL/manifest failure
(and comparison/audit I/O errors); 4 audit findings at error level; 5 comparison
mismatch; 6 xref-summary failure; 7 index-pattern failure; 8 data-consumer
failure; 9 data-coverage failure. An assembly error can take precedence over
a later diagnostic-listing error. Any nonzero status means the invocation failed.
These status codes describe xasm. xlnk also returns a nonzero status on input,
write, close, or publication errors. A linker script publishes each completed
file when it switches outputs; a later failure does not roll back earlier files.
Files used by `copy` must retain their size during linking, including files
replaced by earlier output commands. A size change fails the current output
because it would invalidate the addresses calculated during layout.

Listing version 2 contract
--------------------------

    xasm --pure-binary game.asm -o game.prg \
      --listing=game.listing.json --listing-format=json --listing-version=2

JSON keeps the `source_file`, `output_file`, and `records` envelope, sets
`version` to the string `"2"`, and adds `binary_generated`. NDJSON has one
record per line and no envelope. Every version 2 record contains `version`
and `binary_generated`, so streamed records are self-describing too. An empty
NDJSON listing has no records. Object output is unsupported in version 2:
its serialized object-file offsets are not raw emitted-byte offsets. Invalid
version values or version 2 without a structured listing and `--pure-binary`
are command-line errors (exit 2).
An explicit `--listing-version=1` without `--listing` warns with W0017,
like `--listing-format`; `--no-warn` suppresses this warning.

Rows retain the source fields (`file`, `line`, `column`, `source_text`),
`directive_or_opcode`, `addressing_mode`, and `continuation_of_record`.
For instructions, version 2's `addressing_mode` describes the final opcode,
including zero-page shortening during layout, and agrees with instruction
records. Version 1 retains its historical parsed-mode metadata.
Data and included binaries remain split into rows of at most four bytes;
the continuation flag marks subsequent rows of the same statement. Labels,
segment switches, and `.ORG` have zero length. Segment-switch rows describe
the newly selected segment; `.ORG` rows describe the new CPU address.

| Field | Version 2 meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `segment` | `"code"` or `"data"`, independently on every row. |
| `byte_count` | Number of CPU address-space bytes occupied by this row, including reserved storage. Zero for labels and control directives. |
| `cpu_address_start`, `cpu_address_end` | Inclusive CPU span as `"0xHHHH"` strings, modulo 65536. For zero-length rows both contain the current address. |
| `binary_generated` | Whether this invocation successfully published a new binary. Does not imply that all later analysis/export operations succeeded. |
| `emits_bytes` | True only for a positive-length code-segment row when `binary_generated` is true. |
| `output_offset_start`, `output_offset_end` | Inclusive, zero-based byte offsets in that binary if `emits_bytes` is true; otherwise both are `null`. |
| `bytes_hex` | Actual emitted bytes as two-digit uppercase hex strings; empty for non-emitting rows and compressed zero-fill storage. |
| `fill_byte` | `"00"` for emitting storage rows, repeated `byte_count` times; otherwise `null`. |

All data-segment rows have `emits_bytes: false`, null output offsets, and no
bytes, even when their source has explicit data or `.INCBIN`. They retain
their CPU spans. A storage directive has one row covering its complete span;
code storage describes zero fill without materializing a large byte array.
This also covers late label-difference counts once binary layout is resolved.
Text and version 1 listings intentionally omit storage bytes; use version 2
for complete binary reconstruction, including zero fill.
These rules also apply to padding generated from user-defined types.

Join binary offsets to rows with `emits_bytes: true` using the inclusive
output interval. These intervals partition the generated binary, including
storage; `.ORG` changes CPU addresses without inserting file bytes. Join
instruction records by their `output_offset` against these intervals.
Instruction records retain CPU addresses above `$FFFF` unwrapped; compare
them modulo 65536 to listing CPU addresses, or join by output offset.
CPU addresses alone are not unique: code and data can overlap, and `.ORG`
can reuse addresses. Include `segment` in memory joins and retain all matches
when addresses are reused. Labels have no output interval; associate them
with spans by segment and CPU address, accounting for that ambiguity.

A diagnostic listing after an assembly or binary-output error has
`binary_generated: false`: every row has null output offsets, empty bytes,
null fill, and `emits_bytes: false`. CPU spans in such a listing are advisory.
An existing binary from a previous invocation is never claimed as this
invocation's output. As with version 1, some early failures cannot produce a
listing; callers must check exit status and avoid using stale artifacts.

Version 2 uses the existing listing traversal and byte evaluation. Added work
is constant per row; storage is represented in constant space. Default-format
output must remain byte-for-byte compatible, and real-project timing must
meet the performance limits below. Tests reconstruct binaries from version 2
rows, verify joins and JSON/NDJSON equivalence, and preserve version 1 coverage.

Performance
-----------

A change must not noticeably slow assembly or analysis. Before merging a change
to the assembler or its outputs, compare it with the base branch on the largest
available real project:

    python3 tests/verify_fceux_projects.py --baseline /path/to/base/xasm \
      --candidate ./xasm --source /path/to/largest.asm --report /tmp/xasm-check.json

Add `--allow-analysis-changes` for an intended output change. The median CPU
time of every timed mode may grow by at most 5%, plus 20 ms for timer noise.
The report records output sizes; they may grow for new content, not for
repetition. A larger regression needs explicit approval, recorded with its
reason. Specs for new or changed outputs state their expected cost and the
measurements that confirm it.

Maintainer builds
-----------------

`version.h` is the version source for both tools and configure. After changing
it, regenerate configure with Autoconf 2.73; regenerate Makefile.in with
Automake 1.19 after Makefile.am changes. Keep those generated files in commits.
Use Bison 3.8.2 and Flex 2.6.4 for parser/lexer regeneration. Ordinary builds
should leave maintainer mode disabled. Regenerate the HTML manual with
Texinfo 7.3 after editing xorcyst.texinfo:

    makeinfo --html --split=chapter --output=doc xorcyst.texinfo

Regenerate `man1/xasm.1` and `man1/xlnk.1` with help2man 1.49.3 after changing
either tool's `--help`/`--version` output or the `man1/*.h2m` include files
(SEE ALSO/COPYRIGHT material not derivable from `--help`). This happens
automatically as part of an `--enable-maintainer-mode` build; both man pages
are also checked in, so ordinary builds and `make distcheck` (which forces
`HELP2MAN=:`) never require help2man to be installed.

The manual version must match `version.h`; installation checks enforce this.

Real-project compatibility checks are opt-in and keep project inputs read-only:

    python3 tests/verify_fceux_projects.py \
      --baseline /path/to/previous/xasm --candidate ./xasm \
      --source /path/to/project/asm/project.asm --report /tmp/xasm-check.json

The report compares binary bytes, diagnostics, and analysis outputs across
eight modes with NL disabled/enabled, and records output sizes and paired
timing samples.

To prepare and validate a source archive (including tests and feature specs):

    ./configure --disable-maintainer-mode
    make distcheck

This produces `xorcyst-1.9.10.tar.gz`. No parser or scanner regeneration is
needed to build the archive. The installation checks use a temporary prefix;
they do not replace the system installation.
